.\" Manpage for ovn-analyzer.
.TH OVN-ANALYZER 1 "2026-08-29" "ovn-offline-tool" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ovn-analyzer \- offline, host-native inspector for extracted OVN databases
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ovn-analyzer
.BI \-\-nb " ovnnb_db.db"
.BI \-\-sb " ovnsb_db.db"
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.br
.B ovn-analyzer
.B compare
.I dumpdirA dumpdirB
.br
.B ovn-analyzer
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ovn-analyzer
serves OVN Northbound and Southbound database files that were extracted from a
cluster (typically from the
.B ovnkube-node
pods of an OpenShift / Kubernetes cluster) and runs the standard OVN client
tools against them entirely offline.
.PP
On each invocation it creates a private temporary directory under
.IR /tmp ,
copies the supplied database files into it, starts one detached
.BR ovsdb-server (1)
per database on a unique Unix domain socket, and exports
.B OVN_NB_DB
and
.B OVN_SB_DB
so that
.BR ovn-nbctl (8),
.BR ovn-sbctl (8),
and
.BR ovn-trace (8)
operate against the offline copies. Clustered (Raft) databases are converted to
standalone automatically with
.BR "ovsdb-tool cluster-to-standalone" .
The original files are never modified.
.PP
All transient servers and temporary files are removed automatically when the
command exits or is interrupted
.RB ( EXIT ,
.BR SIGINT ,
.BR SIGTERM ,
.BR SIGHUP ,
.BR SIGPIPE ,
.BR SIGQUIT ).
.PP
.B ovn-analyzer
is designed for OVN-Kubernetes Interconnect (IC) clusters, in which every node
maintains its own Northbound and Southbound database. Each database pair
therefore describes a single node.
.SH REQUIRED ARGUMENTS
.TP
.BI \-\-nb " path"
Path to the extracted OVN Northbound database file. Required for all commands
except
.BR compare .
.TP
.BI \-\-sb " path"
Path to the extracted OVN Southbound database file. Required for all commands
except
.BR compare .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-keep
Do not delete the
.I /tmp
runtime directory on exit. Useful for debugging the transient servers.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Run the requested command even if the NB/SB pairing checklist (see
.BR check )
fails.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print verbose diagnostics to standard error.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "nbctl " "args..."
Run
.BR ovn-nbctl (8)
against the Northbound database. Any
.B ovn-nbctl
sub-command is accepted, for example
.BR show ,
.BR "list " \fItable\fR,
.BR "find " \fItable\ column=value\fR.
.TP
.BI "sbctl " "args..."
Run
.BR ovn-sbctl (8)
against the Southbound database, for example
.BR show ,
.BR lflow-list ,
.BR "list Chassis" .
.TP
.BI "trace " "args..."
Run
.BR ovn-trace (8)
against the Southbound database to perform an offline packet trace, for example
.RB \(dq "trace \-\-detailed " \fIdatapath\fR " '" \fImicroflow\fR "'" \(dq.
.TP
.BI "appctl " "nb|sb args..."
Run
.BR ovs-appctl (8)
against the control (unixctl) socket of the Northbound or Southbound transient
server, for example
.RB \(dq "appctl nb memory/show" \(dq
or
.RB \(dq "appctl sb ovsdb-server/list-dbs" \(dq.
.TP
.B check
Run only the NB/SB pairing checklist and exit. Verifies that
.B \-\-nb
is an
.B OVN_Northbound
database, that
.B \-\-sb
is an
.B OVN_Southbound
database, and that both belong to the same node (compared via
.B NB_Global.options:name
and
.BR SB_Global.options:name ).
Exits non-zero on any mismatch.
.TP
.BI "dump " "[OUTDIR]"
Snapshot the node's Northbound and Southbound databases into normalized,
UUID-scrubbed, sorted files suitable for stable diffing. The default
.I OUTDIR
is
.IB ./ovn-dump- <node-label>\fR.
.TP
.BI "compare " "dumpdirA dumpdirB"
Diff two directories produced by
.BR dump .
This command is fully offline and needs neither
.B \-\-nb
nor
.BR \-\-sb .
Differences in tables that OVN-Kubernetes keeps identical across every IC node
.RB ( Address_Set ,
.BR Port_Group ,
.BR Chassis )
are reported as drift; per-node differences are reported as expected.
.TP
.B interactive
Spawn a subshell in which
.BR OVN_NB_DB ,
.BR OVN_SB_DB ,
.BR OVN_NB_CTL ,
and
.B OVN_SB_CTL
are pre-exported, along with the
.BR nbctl ,
.BR sbctl ,
.BR trace ,
.BR appctl-nb ,
and
.B appctl-sb
aliases. Exit the subshell to tear down the transient servers.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OVN_NB_DB
Set by the tool to the Northbound transient server socket
.RB ( "unix:" ...).
.TP
.B OVN_SB_DB
Set by the tool to the Southbound transient server socket.
.TP
.B OVN_NB_CTL
.TQ
.B OVN_SB_CTL
Exported in
.B interactive
mode; paths to the Northbound / Southbound control (unixctl) sockets.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or a consistent result from
.B check
/
.BR compare .
.TP
.B 1
General error, or a failed pairing checklist.
.TP
.B 2
.B compare
detected drift in an invariant (cluster-synced) table.
.TP
.B 127
A required OVN binary was not found on the host.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /tmp/ovn-analyzer.XXXXXX
Per-invocation runtime directory holding the served database copies, Unix
sockets, pidfiles, and server logs. Removed on exit unless
.B \-\-keep
is given.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Verify a pair belongs to the same node:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ovn-analyzer \-\-nb node_ovnnb_db.db \-\-sb node_ovnsb_db.db check
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the Northbound topology and the compiled Southbound flows:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ovn-analyzer \-\-nb node_ovnnb_db.db \-\-sb node_ovnsb_db.db nbctl show
ovn-analyzer \-\-nb node_ovnnb_db.db \-\-sb node_ovnsb_db.db sbctl lflow-list
.EE
.RE
.PP
Trace a broadcast packet:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ovn-analyzer \-\-nb node_ovnnb_db.db \-\-sb node_ovnsb_db.db \e
    trace \-\-detailed ls1 'inport=="vm1" && eth.dst==ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Dump two nodes and compare them:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ovn-analyzer \-\-nb a_ovnnb_db.db \-\-sb a_ovnsb_db.db dump ./dumpA
ovn-analyzer \-\-nb b_ovnnb_db.db \-\-sb b_ovnsb_db.db dump ./dumpB
ovn-analyzer compare ./dumpA ./dumpB
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ovn-nbctl (8),
.BR ovn-sbctl (8),
.BR ovn-trace (8),
.BR ovsdb-server (1),
.BR ovsdb-tool (1),
.BR ovs-appctl (8)
.SH NOTES
The host OVN client version should match the cluster's OVN version where
possible. A host client older than the captured database schema may print a
harmless warning about a missing column (for example
.IR "lacks nb_uuid column" ).
.SH AUTHOR
Ketan Lakhwara.
